uard**\n\n- For each step of your rule:\n    - **Ask:** Would this introduce or omit any structure, shift, alignment, or pattern not seen in every training output?\n    - **If so, revise** the rule to avoid overgeneralizing\u2014**mirror the specific arrangements and transformations demonstrated**, and do not assume cropping, left-justifying, canonical paths, or generic minimal solutions unless unambiguously present in all training examples.\n\n---\n\n### **4. Implementation and Detailed Output Verification**\n\n- When generating outputs:\n    - Use your stepwise rule **as written**.  \n    - Check that every shape, pattern, and color/label is present **at exactly the locations and extents shown in the training outputs**.\n    - **Do not shift, realign, crop, fill, or generalize** unless this behavior is supported by *all* examples.\n\n- **Cross-validate:**  \n    - Visually or programmatically compare your output grid to the expected sample for every position.\n    - If there is **any mismatch** (shape, placement, content, or size), **diagnose exactly what differs** and return to review or refine your rule until a perfect match is achieved.\n\n---\n\n**Core Principle:**  \nLet the specifics of the training outputs dictate your logic. ARC solutions nearly always require reproducing the *given* spatial structure, transformations, and arrangements\u2014rather than inferring a minimal, canonical, or compressed version. **When facing ambiguity or possible interpretations, always default to recreating the outputs as shown.**\n\n**Quick self-check before finalizing:**  \nFor each decision, ask: \"*Could this action add, omit, shift, or generalize anything not directly present in every example?*\"  \n- If yes: **change your approach to strictly follow the evidence in the training pairs.**\n\n---\n\n**Your final deliverable:**  \n- Write your transformation rule, clearly and stepwise, referencing both feature identity and position.\n- Construct outputs that **exactly** matc